ROOT=Path('/mnt/data/MEPS_methods_code_audit_2026-10-28')
MAIN=Path('/mnt/data/MEPS_article_matched_supplement_style/Depression_Remission_Main_Article_Matching_Supplement.docx')
SUP=Path('/mnt/data/Depression_Remission_Technical_Supplement_Cited.docx')

def replace_primary_run(p, new):
    # Existing referenced paragraphs use a main prose run followed by a superscript citation run.
    # Preserve the existing citations and paragraph styling.
    main=next((r for r in p.runs if r.text and not r.font.superscript),None)
    assert main is not None,(p.style.name,p.text[:50])
    main.text=new
    for r in p.runs:
        if r._element is not main._element and not r.font.superscript:
            r.text=''

main_replacements={
12:'The proposed policy outcome is clinical depression remission at approximately 12 months. MEPS provides the PHQ-2, for which a score of 3 or greater indicates a positive symptom screen and a score below 3 indicates a negative screen. The empirical work did not identify PHQ-9-confirmed clinical remission. Annual expenditure models compared current positive and negative PHQ-2 screens, regardless of previous depression history; the separate four-wave data provided symptom-screen transitions. No group with documented clinical remission and five observed postremission expenditure years was analyzed.',
13:'For each payer, we modeled the association between current PHQ-2 screen status and annual medical expenditures using weighted two-part models: logistic regression for any expenditure and a Gamma log-link model for positive expenditure amounts. The models adjusted for age and age squared, sex, race, poverty, education, insurance, census region, and year. Both predicted screen states were standardized to adults currently PHQ-2-positive in the payer population. Importantly, the screen-negative observations used to fit these models included people without a previously observed positive screen. Estimates were expressed in 2024 dollars; intervention spending was not separately identified.',
15:'We modeled a hypothetical outcome landmark approximately 12 months after initial positive screening; exact clinical assessment dates were not identified in the expenditure data. Starting after that landmark, one projected trajectory was initialized as screen-negative and the comparison as screen-positive. Four-wave MEPS transition rates, assumed constant over time, determined subsequent screen-state differences. A five-year projection used annual starting-state rewards and 3% real midyear discounting, not five observed years of postremission costs. We applied an external 53% untreated-depression remission estimate as a fixed policy pricing adjustment, then 100%, 75%, or 50% recognition. Neither the 53% discount nor recognition percentages estimated intervention-attributable effects.',
17:'The expenditure regressions used MEPS self-administered-questionnaire weights; uncertainty was calculated separately using strata- and primary-sampling-unit-aggregated influence functions. The longitudinal transitions used the four-wave SAQ weight and pooled variance-design identifiers. Annual contrasts had normal-approximation 95% survey intervals. Five-year conditional intervals propagated estimated expenditure and transition covariances through 120,000 multivariate-normal simulations, assuming no cross-source covariance and holding the 53% factor fixed. Numerical convergence, selected gradient components, alternative model specification, and transition arithmetic were checked. No external predictive validation or causal effect estimation was performed.',
25:'The four-wave analysis included 4,496 adults. The weighted next-year positive-screen probability was 48.8% among currently screen-positive transition observations and 23.7% among screen-negative observations with any earlier positive screen. Of 444 such previously-positive/screen-negative transition observations, 101 followed another screen-negative wave rather than an immediately preceding positive screen. Their projected difference yielded 1.302 discounted state-years over five subsequent years. With the fixed 53% policy adjustment, the full model-based credit was $5,200 for Medicare and $4,127 for all medical expenditures. These are projected symptom-state valuations, not direct measurements of clinical-remission savings.',
32:'All four primary two-part models converged. A separately fitted single-part Poisson pseudo-maximum-likelihood model on the same annual observations produced annual contrasts of $7,222 overall, $8,071 for Medicare, $3,345 for Medicaid, and $3,064 for private insurance. A descriptive two-year MEPS record check yielded 46.4% positive-screen persistence, compared with 48.8% in the four-wave analysis; the samples overlapped and this was not independent validation. An earlier prospective remission-specific MEPS analysis, not used to construct the final rate card, instead estimated an imprecise negative Medicare annual contrast of -$7,141 (95% CI, -$17,542 to $3,260). The different results reflect different estimands and remain unresolved (Supplement and audit).',
34:'This decision-analytic model generated positive five-year expenditure valuations by extrapolating adjusted cross-sectional symptom-screen spending differences. These values were not observed in a five-year clinical-remission cohort, and an earlier prospective remission-specific analysis yielded an imprecise Medicare comparison of the opposite sign. Accordingly, the resulting $5,200, $3,900, and $2,600 Medicare amounts are conditional policy-pricing scenarios, not demonstrated savings from clinical remission. A fixed credit remains a possible policy mechanism, but these numerical rates require direct postremission validation.',
38:'The annual cost comparison was between currently screen-positive and screen-negative adults; many screen-negative observations were not known to have had depression, and model standardization does not identify a remitter-specific causal effect. The four-wave transition model included some screen-negative observations beyond the first year of resolution, and five future years were projected rather than observed. The two-year transition check reused some four-wave respondents, while the independent PPML specification used the same annual observations. The 53% adjustment was held fixed, and the uncertainty intervals omit structural, clinical-measure, and unmeasured-confounding uncertainty. The previously conducted prospective Medicare contrast also had an imprecise opposite sign.',
40:'Using observed PHQ-2 screen-state expenditure associations, four-wave screen transitions, and a fixed 53% policy adjustment, we calculated a provisional five-year rate-card scenario: $5,200 for Medicare at 100% recognition, $3,900 at 75%, and $2,600 at 50%. Because a more directly prospective MEPS comparison differs materially, these amounts should not be presented as validated postremission savings or adopted as actuarially established clinical-remission credits without linked clinical and longitudinal claims validation.'
}

def replace_label_body(p,new):
    runs=[r for r in p.runs if r.text]
    assert len(runs)>=2,p.text[:70]
    runs[-1].text=new

main=Document(MAIN)
for i,v in main_replacements.items():replace_primary_run(main.paragraphs[i],v)
# Maintain the abstract and Key Points while preventing the front page from implying remitter-specific costs.
abstract=main.tables[1].cell(0,0)
replace_label_body(abstract.paragraphs[4], '  Current depressive symptom state was classified with the 2-item Patient Health Questionnaire (PHQ-2). A positive-to-negative transition was observed only for the longitudinal screening transition model; the annual expenditure regressions did not follow identified remitters.')
replace_label_body(abstract.paragraphs[7], '  The models produced a reproducible provisional symptom-screen rate card, not a directly estimated value of clinical remission. An earlier prospective remitter-specific analysis yielded a discordant and imprecise Medicare estimate. Prospective clinical and claims-based validation is necessary before using these rates to support payment policy.')
kp=main.tables[1].cell(0,1)
replace_label_body(kp.paragraphs[3], '\nThe calculation is a reproducible symptom-screen valuation scenario. It is not demonstrated clinical-remission savings: a separate prospective analysis produced a conflicting, imprecise Medicare result.')
main_target=ROOT/'AUDITED_CORRECTED_Main_Article.docx'
main.save(main_target)

supp_replacements={
5:'Adults aged 18-85 years were eligible for annual expenditure modeling with PHQ-2 in 0-6, positive SAQ weight, required age/sex/race/poverty/coverage/region codes, and nonnegative spending. Two observations (one in 2019 and one in 2021) with region coded -1 were excluded by this validity rule; the final scripts do not preserve a separate pre-exclusion separation-model result. Education with negative or missing coding was retained as an unknown group. PHQ-2 >=3 defined a positive screen, not a clinical diagnosis. The annual negative-screen sample was not restricted to people with past depression or a documented remission. MEPS does not supply PHQ-9 remission or the CMS 12-month assessment window.',
19:'The special four-wave analysis required PHQ-2 scores from rounds 2, 4, 6, and 8, valid demographic/insurance variables, a positive four-wave SAQ weight, and nonnegative expenditure variables in all four annual waves. Those longitudinal expenditure variables were eligibility filters only; postremission expenditures from these panels were not used to estimate the headline annual cost contrast. The final eligible longitudinal population comprised 4,496 adults. A positive-state (P) transition was one from a current PHQ-2 score >=3. A screen-negative-with-history state (R) required current PHQ-2 <3 and any observed earlier positive score, not necessarily a new positive-to-negative change in the immediately preceding year. Across people and waves there were 1,505 eligible transitions of these two types among 13,488 adjacent-wave pairs; multiple transitions from a respondent were combined in a respondent-level influence function.',
20:'Weighted transition estimates were a = Pr(P next year | currently screen-negative, any earlier positive) = 0.2373854 and b = Pr(P next year | currently screen-positive) = 0.4884412. The projected two-state matrix T = [[1-a,a],[1-b,b]] (state order R,P) was an assumption imposed after a hypothetical 12-month landmark, with one trajectory initialized at R and its comparator at P. The model contrast in expected positive-state probabilities in future year k is (b-a)^(k-1). This was not an observed five-year trajectory, and the R-to-P transition observations were not restricted to the first year after newly documented remission.',
26:'Descriptive, overlapping two-year record check: among 2,430 PHQ-2-positive paired annual observations, 1,166 had a positive screen in the next year (weighted 46.35%), compared with 48.84% in the four-wave analysis. These pairs were assembled from the same pooled annual MEPS sources; 870 came from people who also met the four-wave analytic eligibility criteria. Consequently this was not an independent-sample or external validation. The two-year pairing also cannot identify previous positive history among all currently negative respondents.',
30:'The code drew 120,000 multivariate-normal replicates for the four annual spending contrasts, retaining estimated cross-payer survey covariance. A second bivariate-normal draw represented the two longitudinal transition rates, with each simulated rate clipped between 0 and 1. The two draws were treated as independent by setting cross-source covariance to zero even though respondents in the underlying MEPS sources can overlap; this covariance was not estimated. The five-year factor and rate were recomputed for each draw, and the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles define conditional uncertainty intervals. The fixed 53% factor, outcome mapping, causal identification, and projection structure were not included as uncertain parameters.',
37:'Implemented code checks included uniqueness of annual person-year records, nonnegative payment outcomes, a $5-tolerance check that selected payer components did not exceed total spending, model-matrix rank, convergence, positive/zero spending support, Hessian condition number <1e11, nonnegative payable credit candidates, and exact 75%/50% arithmetic. The analytic derivatives of standardized predictions were spot-checked by finite differences for three selected coefficients per GLM component, rather than for every coefficient. Full survey variance used original strata and PSU rosters with zero-contribution domain PSUs. The final code excluded two annual observations with invalid region codes; it does not include the pre-exclusion regression or the historical separation diagnostic. These are implementation checks, not proof of unbiased effect estimation or out-of-sample predictive validity.',
38:'A separately fitted PPML log-link mean model used the same annual observations, covariates, and standardization target as the primary two-part model. Its similar positive contrasts provided an alternate-specification diagnostic, not independent-data or causal validation. The five-year formula was checked against matrix multiplication with the estimated transition rates and 25 random rate combinations. The independently repeated primary run reproduced all 10 archived aggregate CSV/JSON outputs byte-for-byte. An earlier, separately reproducible prospective remitter-versus-persister analysis used 690 eligible positive-screen person-transitions (526 people) and measured expenditures after the assessment year; its Medicare annual difference was -$7,141 (95% CI, -$17,542 to $3,260). This imprecise discordant result was not used to construct the positive primary rate card and must accompany its actuarial interpretation.',
44:'The final rate-card estimates are adjusted annual associations between current PHQ-2 screen states followed by a projected five-year state-difference calculation; they are not observed medical savings after clinical remission. The annual screen-negative regression comparison included respondents with no observed depression history. Marginal standardization to screen-positive profiles cannot remove this structural difference. A separate analysis of observed PHQ-2 positive-to-negative versus persistently positive transitions and subsequent expenditures found a Medicare annual contrast of -$7,141 (95% CI, -$17,542 to $3,260), rather than the positive cross-sectional state association used in the headline. This disagreement is consequential to policy, and neither PPML agreement with the primary cross-sectional model nor computational reproducibility resolves it.',
63:'Statistical uncertainty. Analytic design-based influence functions and 120,000 normal draws propagated observed sampling variation in annual state-spending contrasts and symptom-transition rates. Annual and longitudinal sources were assigned zero cross-covariance for simulation, even though underlying respondents can overlap. The fixed 53% adjustment, selection and unmeasured confounding, outcome misclassification, intervention effects, and uncertainty in the state-projection architecture were not modeled. These intervals and exceedance probabilities are not financial-breakeven estimates.',
}
